introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we organise data so we can find it quickly and use it efficiently?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on the transition from single-item variables to <strong>collections</strong>. You will explore how data is structured in memory to improve efficiency in both time (speed of access) and space (memory usage). Key paradigms include <strong>Static</strong> vs <strong>Dynamic</strong> structures, and the rules of <strong>LIFO</strong> and <strong>FIFO</strong>."
  journey: "You'll begin by comparing rigid <strong>arrays</strong> with flexible <strong>lists</strong>. Then, you'll master two fundamental abstract data types: the <strong>Stack</strong> (like a pile of trays) and the <strong>Queue</strong> (like a line for lunch), learning how to manipulate them and apply them to real-world problems."

concepts:
  - title: "Static vs Dynamic Structures"
    icon: "fa-memory"
    content: |
      <p>Data structures differ in how they manage memory:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Static (e.g., Array):</strong> Fixed size, contiguous memory (items next to each other). Fast access but wasteful if not full.</li>
          <li><strong>Dynamic (e.g., List):</strong> Resizable, non-contiguous memory. Flexible but can be slower to access specific items directly.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "The Stack (LIFO)"
    icon: "fa-layer-group"
    content: |
      <p>A <strong>Stack</strong> is a LIFO (Last In, First Out) structure. Imagine a stack of plates — you can only add or remove the top one.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Push:</strong> Add an item to the top.</li>
          <li><strong>Pop:</strong> Remove the top item.</li>
          <li><strong>Peek:</strong> Look at the top item without removing it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Real-world use:</em> The "Undo" button in your text editor.</p>

  - title: "The Queue (FIFO)"
    icon: "fa-people-arrows"
    content: |
      <p>A <strong>Queue</strong> is a FIFO (First In, First Out) structure. Imagine a queue for a bus — the first person to arrive is the first to board.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Enqueue:</strong> Add an item to the rear.</li>
          <li><strong>Dequeue:</strong> Remove an item from the front.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Real-world use:</em> A printer buffer (first document sent is first printed).</p>

  - title: "1D & 2D Lists"
    icon: "fa-table-cells"
    content: |
      <p>We use lists to store multiple related items under one name. A <strong>2D List</strong> (or Matrix) is a list of lists, useful for grids, game boards, or images.</p>
      <div class="warning-box">
          <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> 2D Indices Trick</h4>
          <p>Remember <strong>"RC Cola"</strong>: Row then Column. <code>grid[row][col]</code> accesses the item at that position.</p>
      </div>

terminology:
  - term: "Static Structure"
    definition: "A data structure with a fixed size determined at compile time (e.g., an array)."
  - term: "Dynamic Structure"
    definition: "A data structure that can grow or shrink in size during execution (e.g., a linked list or Python list)."
  - term: "LIFO"
    definition: "Last In, First Out. The principle used by Stacks."
  - term: "FIFO"
    definition: "First In, First Out. The principle used by Queues."
  - term: "Push / Pop"
    definition: "Operations to add/remove items from the TOP of a stack."
  - term: "Enqueue / Dequeue"
    definition: "Operations to add to the REAR and remove from the FRONT of a queue."
  - term: "Pointer"
    definition: "A variable that stores the memory address of another value (used to track Top/Front/Rear)."
  - term: "Overflow"
    definition: "Attempting to push an item onto a full stack."
  - term: "Underflow"
    definition: "Attempting to pop an item from an empty stack."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Direct vs Sequential"
    correction: "Thinking you can 'jump' to any item in a dynamic list instantly. Often, the computer must walk through the list item-by-item to find it."
  - mistake: "Stack Direction"
    correction: "Trying to remove items from the bottom of specific stacks. LIFO means the bottom is stuck until the top is gone!"
  - mistake: "2D Array Indices"
    correction: "Confusing [x][y] with [row][col]. In programming, we usually index by [row] (vertical position) then [col] (horizontal position)."

